PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Round 0 shows strong majority (Colleagues 1,3-7,10-12,14-16,18) converging on E: Level-1 trauma center (15 min) with on-call toxicologist is optimal for blunt traumatic arrest + acetaminophen OD. Core rationale: arrest from fall requires highest-capability trauma resources (24/7 in-house surgery, OR, thoracotomy, MTP) per ATLS/PHTLS; tox (NAC, liver support) is secondary but additive value in E; 15 min transport acceptable under CPR. 

Dissent: Colleague 17 selects C (closest Level-2, 8 min) citing time dominance and tox irrelevance in acute arrest; Colleague 8 echoes transport-time priority over 15 min options; Colleague 2 uniquely weights immediate tox expertise highest; Colleague 13 undecided pending tradeoff review. 

Key claims preserved: acetaminophen does not drive immediate arrest (delayed hepatotoxicity); lower-level centers (A-C) lack full trauma capability and risk later transfer; no major factual errors noted. Unresolved: exact weight of tox vs. pure trauma level when times differ. My current stance aligns with E majority.}

15.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Majority (Colleagues 1,3,4,5,6,7,9,10,11,12,14,15,16,18) select E: Level-1 trauma center 15 min away with toxicologist. Core reasoning: traumatic arrest (blunt 3-story fall) requires highest-capability trauma resources (24/7 in-house surgery, thoracotomy, MTP, OR) per ATLS/PHTLS; acetaminophen OD is secondary (delayed hepatotoxicity, NAC later) but benefits from on-call tox at same site. 15 min transport acceptable during CPR; lower-level centers (A–C) lack resources and risk later transfer. 

Minority dissent: Colleague 17 (and 8) favor C (closest Level-2, 8 min) because arrest demands minimal low-flow time; tox irrelevant acutely. Colleague 2 over-weights immediate tox expertise. Colleague 13 defers. 

Unresolved: exact weight of transport time vs. capability in dual trauma+tox arrest; whether acetaminophen history alters destination when arrest timing implicates trauma. No factual errors identified. My view aligns with E but notes time-sensitivity trade-off needs monitoring in future rounds.}

