PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Majority (now 1-7,9-18; 17 switched) selects E: Level-1 + tox 15 min. Core: blunt traumatic arrest (3-story fall) requires Level-1 resources (24/7 in-house surgery, thoracotomy, MTP, OR per ATLS/PHTLS); acetaminophen OD secondary/delayed (NAC post-ROSC/teleconsult) but benefits from integrated on-call tox at same site. 15 min CPR transport acceptable; A-C lack trauma capability (risk secondary transfer), D only Level 2. 

Minority time concern (prior C17/C8) largely resolved—C17 now E; C8 concedes tox can complicate ACLS/acidosis but still notes low-flow trade-off. Col 2/5/13/14 continue weighting immediate tox integration highest. No new factual errors. 

Unresolved: precise physiologic weight of acetaminophen in refractory arrest vs pure trauma; exact time-vs-capability threshold in dual pathology. My view remains E but time-sensitivity flagged for monitoring.}

